      "id": "word-lo--lo-que-intensity",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "lo",
      "answerEn": "no separate English word; helps lo que mean “how much”",
      "noteEn": "In an emphatic lo que followed by a verb, lo does not mean “it” or “the thing.” Together with que, it points to the amount or intensity of the action.",
      "example": {
        "es": "¡No sabes lo que llovió!",
        "en": "You have no idea how much it rained!"
      },
      "tags": ["word", "intensity", "quantitative expression"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-que--lo-que-intensity",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "que",
      "answerEn": "no separate English word; completes the intensifying lo que, “how much”",
      "noteEn": "Together with lo before a verb, que can help emphasize how much an action happens or how strong its effect is. This is different from lo que meaning “what” or “the thing that.”",
      "example": {
        "es": "¡No sabes lo que llovió!",
        "en": "You have no idea how much it rained!"
      },
      "tags": ["word", "intensity", "quantitative expression"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-me--experiencer-doler",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "me",
      "answerEn": "to me; marks the person who feels the pain",
      "noteEn": "Doler is organized differently from English “hurt”: the painful thing controls the verb, while me, te, or le identifies who feels it.",
      "example": {
        "es": "Me duele la espalda.",
        "en": "My back hurts."
      },
      "tags": ["word", "doler", "experiencer", "pain"]
